    "body": "\n\n## Overview\n\nLets out-of-tree platforms register block-scaled FP8 linear kernels by adding `kernel_type=\"fp8_block\"` to `register_linear_kernel`.\n\n## Claims\n\n- `register_linear_kernel(..., kernel_type=\"fp8_block\")` adds the kernel to `_POSSIBLE_FP8_BLOCK_KERNELS`.\n- OOT platforms can now serve `--quantization fp8_per_block`, which previously failed during kernel selection because the OOT platform had no entry in the block-kernel list.\n- The docstring now lists every `kernel_type` the function accepts. It previously listed only `mp`, `int8` and `fp8`.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `pre-commit` passes (ruff, mypy, typos, SPDX).\n- The change adds one `elif` branch that matches the existing `fp8`, `mxfp8`, `nvfp4`, `mxfp4` and `mxfp6` branches. Existing kernel types behave as before.\n\n## Details\n\n`Fp8PerBlockOnlineLinearMethod` (and `Fp8LinearMethod` with `block_quant`) use a `GroupShape(1, 128)` activation key. Because `is_per_group()` is true for that shape, `init_fp8_linear_kernel` picks the kernel from `_POSSIBLE_FP8_BLOCK_KERNELS`. That dict only has entries for CUDA, ROCm, CPU and XPU, and `register_linear_kernel` had no way to add to it. An OOT platform that registered its FP8 kernel with `kernel_type=\"fp8\"` therefore got per-tensor and per-channel FP8 but never block-wise.\n\nTo support both, an OOT plugin registers the same class twice, once as `\"fp8\"` and once as `\"fp8_block\"`. If that class subclasses `FP8ScaledMMLinearKernel`, the block path already builds it with `layer_param_names` (see the `issubclass` branch in `init_fp8_linear_kernel`), so no new base class or interface change is needed.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [ ] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n\n",
